--- what it costs at import time ---
  import openai           2.42s (cold process)
  import langgraph.graph  3.04s (cold process)

--- what it costs per run ---
  v0.9  :   6.2s median  (range 5.6-6.5s)   2 calls, 1,640 tokens per run
          of which 2.7s was spent waiting for the model
  v0.10 :   6.0s median  (range 5.7-6.3s)   2 calls, 1,642 tokens per run
          of which 2.6s was spent waiting for the model

  spread within one version : 1.0s
  gap between the versions  : 0.1s
  ranges overlap            : yes
  -> the difference is inside the noise

Read the token counts first. They differ by 0.1% - the model wording its
own replies slightly differently between runs, not the framework adding anything.
Who owns the loop does not change what the model is asked, so it cannot change
what the model charges.

Then read the last block. Both versions do the same tool work - one warehouse
query, one retrieval - and both spend most of the remainder waiting for the same
network.

Here the two medians land 0.1s apart while a single version varies by
1.0s across its own 7 runs. The framework's bookkeeping is below the
resolution of the experiment: there is nothing to measure, which is the
answer to the question people usually ask about frameworks and speed.

The price is the second list: sixteen packages with their own release
schedules, their own breaking changes and their own bugs, sitting between your
stack trace and your code. That is the thing to weigh against durable state and
a human in the loop - not milliseconds.

Chapter 17's ninety lines remain the right answer when you need neither.
